MVM.registerModule({
  "id": "m04",
  "summary": "Explain how an internal combustion engine works, name its main parts and what each does, describe the valvetrain and timing drive, and carry out basic inspection and measurement of engine components.",
  "outcomes": ["Explain how combustion pressure becomes rotation, and describe common engine layouts, cylinder numbering and firing order.","Describe the four strokes and the valve events, and calculate swept volume and displacement.","Name the main parts of the block, head, piston assembly and crankshaft, and say what each does.","Describe how the valvetrain works and why valve clearance and valve timing matter.","Compare timing belts and chains, and explain the risk of a broken belt on an interference engine.","Inspect and measure engine parts such as bores, journals and cylinder heads against specification."],
  "cheatsheet": ["Four strokes: intake, compression, power, exhaust. One power stroke per cylinder every two crankshaft turns.","The camshaft turns at half the speed of the crankshaft in a four-stroke engine.","Displacement = π ÷ 4 × bore² × stroke × number of cylinders.","Compression ratio = (swept volume + clearance volume) ÷ clearance volume.","Piston rings seal the cylinder and control oil: top ring seals gas, oil ring scrapes oil.","Check valve clearance with a feeler gauge on a cold engine; too tight can burn valves, too loose is noisy.","On an interference engine a broken timing belt can bend valves when the pistons hit them.","Check cylinder head flatness with a straightedge and feeler gauge before refitting.","Label, bag and tidy parts during dismantling. Reassemble clean and with the right torque."],
  "lessons": [
    {"id": "m04-l1", "title": "How an engine works", "minutes": 5, "body": [
      {"t":"p","text":"An **internal combustion engine** burns fuel inside its cylinders and turns the pressure of the burning gas into rotation. Almost every vehicle in this course has one, so you need to understand how it works before you can repair it."},
      {"t":"h","text":"From fire to rotation"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Air and fuel are mixed and burned inside a **cylinder**. The burning gas expands and pushes the **piston** down. The piston is joined to a **connecting rod**, and the rod turns the **crankshaft**, which changes the up-and-down movement into rotation. The **flywheel** on the end of the crankshaft keeps the engine turning between power pulses and carries the clutch or torque converter."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Engine terms you will use every day"},
      {"t":"table","head":["Term","Meaning"],"rows":[["TDC and BDC","Top dead centre and bottom dead centre: the highest and lowest points of the piston's travel"],["Bore","The diameter of the cylinder"],["Stroke","The distance the piston travels from TDC to BDC. It equals twice the crank throw."],["Swept volume","The volume the piston sweeps in one stroke"],["Clearance volume","The space left above the piston at TDC, the combustion chamber"],["Compression ratio","Total cylinder volume divided by clearance volume"],["Firing order","The sequence in which the cylinders produce a power stroke"],["Engine speed","Revolutions of the crankshaft per minute (rpm)"]],"caption":"These words appear in every manual and every diagnostic chart."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Petrol and diesel in outline"},
      {"t":"p","text":"A **petrol (gasoline)** engine mixes fuel with air and ignites it with a spark plug. A **diesel** engine compresses air so much that it becomes very hot, then sprays fuel into it, and the fuel ignites by itself. Diesel engines are built stronger to take the higher pressures, and they use more compression. Modules 6 and 7 cover the fuel systems."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Engine layouts"},
      {"t":"ul","items":["**Inline:** cylinders in one row. Four-cylinder inline engines are very common because they are simple and compact.","**V engine:** cylinders in two rows at an angle to each other, such as a V6 or V8. The engine is shorter for the same number of cylinders.","**Flat (horizontally opposed):** two rows lying flat on each side of the crankshaft. The engine is low, which suits some sports and small cars."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Cylinder numbering and firing order"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Cylinders are numbered by the manufacturer, usually starting at the end of the engine opposite the flywheel, or as shown in the manual. The **firing order** is the sequence in which the cylinders fire. A typical four-cylinder inline engine fires 1-3-4-2. Firing order is chosen to balance the engine and spread the power pulses evenly. Check the workshop manual for the numbering and firing order of the engine you are working on."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Displacement"},
      {"t":"p","text":"**Swept volume** is the volume the piston sweeps in one stroke. **Engine displacement** is the swept volume of all the cylinders added together, usually given in cubic centimetres (cm³) or litres."},
      {"t":"formula","text":"Swept volume = π ÷ 4 × bore² × stroke","caption":"Multiply by the number of cylinders for the engine displacement. Use the same unit for bore and stroke."},
      {"t":"example","title":"Displacement of a four-cylinder engine","steps":["Bore is 8.0 cm and stroke is 8.0 cm.","Swept volume of one cylinder = 3.1416 ÷ 4 × 8.0² × 8.0 = 402 cm³ (rounded).","Displacement = 4 × 402 = 1,608 cm³, or about 1.6 litres."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Identifying the engine you are working on"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Many manufacturers fit several engines to one model, and parts and specifications differ between them. An **engine code**, a group of letters and numbers, is stamped on the block and printed on the data plate or in the vehicle documents. It tells you the capacity, fuel and often the power rating. Always find the engine code before you order parts or look up a torque or clearance. Compare the code with the VIN, and record both on the job card."},
      {"t":"p","text":"Some engines are described by their **bore and stroke ratio**. A **square** engine has a bore equal to its stroke. An **oversquare** engine has a larger bore than stroke and suits higher revolutions. An **undersquare** engine has a longer stroke, which gives good torque at low speed and is common in diesels."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"key","text":"Compression ratio compares the total cylinder volume with the volume left when the piston is at the top. You calculated it in module 2. A higher ratio gives more power from the same fuel, but puts more stress on the engine."}
    ]},
    {"id": "m04-l2", "title": "The four-stroke cycle", "minutes": 5, "body": [
      {"t":"p","text":"Most vehicle engines are **four-stroke** engines. Each cylinder completes a repeating cycle of four piston movements, called strokes. Understanding this cycle is the foundation for diagnosing almost every engine fault."},
      {"t":"diagram","name":"four-stroke-cycle"},
      {"t":"h","text":"The four strokes"},
      {"t":"ol","items":["**Intake:** the piston moves down, the intake valve opens, and air (and fuel in a petrol engine) is drawn into the cylinder.","**Compression:** both valves close and the piston moves up, squeezing the air or mixture into a small space. Pressure and temperature rise.","**Power:** the mixture burns (petrol: spark; diesel: fuel injected into hot air). The expanding gas forces the piston down. This is the stroke that does the work.","**Exhaust:** the exhaust valve opens and the piston moves up, pushing the burnt gas out."]},
      {"t":"p","text":"The cycle needs two full turns of the crankshaft (720°) for one power stroke in each cylinder. That is why the **camshaft**, which opens the valves, turns at half the speed of the crankshaft."},
      {"t":"h","text":"What happens inside the cylinder"},
      {"t":"table","head":["Stroke","Pressure in a petrol cylinder (approx.)","Temperature","Event"],"rows":[["Intake","Slightly below atmospheric","Moderate","Air and fuel drawn in"],["Compression","About 10 to 15 bar at the end","Several hundred °C at the end","Mixture squeezed"],["Power","Peaks at 40 to 80 bar after ignition","2,000 °C or more at the peak","Burning gas expands and pushes the piston"],["Exhaust","Slightly above atmospheric","Very hot, 600 to 900 °C leaving","Burnt gas pushed out"]],"caption":"Figures vary widely with engine and load. Diesel pressures are much higher."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Valve events and overlap"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Valves do not open and close exactly at the top and bottom of the piston's travel. The intake valve opens slightly before the piston reaches the top of the exhaust stroke, and the exhaust valve closes slightly after. For a short time both valves are open. This is called **valve overlap**. It helps the exhaust leaving the cylinder to pull fresh air in. Overlap is carefully designed, so wrong valve timing causes poor running."},
      {"t":"table","head":["Stroke","Piston","Intake valve","Exhaust valve"],"rows":[["Intake","Moving down","Open","Closed"],["Compression","Moving up","Closed","Closed"],["Power","Moving down","Closed","Closed"],["Exhaust","Moving up","Closed","Open"]],"caption":"Valve positions during the four strokes"},
      {"t":"h","text":"Why four strokes?"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Separate strokes for taking in air and pushing out exhaust give clean breathing and good fuel efficiency. A **two-stroke** engine completes the cycle in one crankshaft turn by combining some of the strokes, so it fires every turn. It is lighter and simpler, but it usually burns oil with the fuel and produces more pollution. Small motorcycles and tools use them, while cars almost always use four-stroke engines."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Petrol and diesel compared"},
      {"t":"table","head":["Feature","Petrol engine","Diesel engine"],"rows":[["What is drawn in","Air and fuel mixture (or air, with fuel injected directly)","Air only"],["Compression ratio","About 9:1 to 13:1","About 15:1 to 22:1"],["Ignition","By a spark plug","By the heat of compressed air (compression ignition)"],["Power control","By a throttle that limits the air","By the amount of fuel injected"],["Typical efficiency","About 30 to 38%","About 35 to 45%"],["Build","Lighter, higher revolutions","Stronger and heavier to take the higher pressure"]]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Valve timing in degrees"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Valve events are given in degrees of crankshaft rotation, before or after top and bottom dead centre. For example, an intake valve may open 10° before TDC and close 40° after BDC, and the exhaust valve may open 40° before BDC and close 10° after TDC. Adding the open periods shows that the valves are open for much longer than 180°. This helps the engine breathe, because the incoming gas keeps moving after the piston has passed BDC. A timing diagram plots these events in a circle and shows the overlap near TDC."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"tip","text":"To remember the order of the strokes say: suck, squeeze, bang, blow. Then name the valve positions for each one without looking at the diagram."}
    ]},
    {"id": "m04-l3", "title": "Torque, power, efficiency and compression ratio", "minutes": 5, "body": [
      {"t":"p","text":"Torque, power, fuel economy and emissions all follow from how the engine turns the energy in its fuel into rotation. This lesson explains the measures used to describe an engine, how they are linked and what limits them. The numbers help you read specifications and understand why an engine with a fault feels the way it does."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Torque and power"},
      {"t":"p","text":"**Torque** is the turning force at the crankshaft, in newton metres (N·m). It is what pushes the vehicle off from rest. **Power** is how quickly work is done, in kilowatts (kW). It sets the top speed and how fast the vehicle can climb or accelerate at speed. Power is torque multiplied by speed, so an engine can have high power with modest torque if it turns fast."},
      {"t":"formula","text":"Power (kW) = torque (N·m) × engine speed (rpm) ÷ 9549"},
      {"t":"example","title":"Comparing a petrol and a diesel engine","steps":["A petrol engine gives 150 N·m at 5,000 rpm. Power = 150 × 5,000 ÷ 9,549 = 78.5 kW.","A diesel engine gives 300 N·m at 2,500 rpm. Power = 300 × 2,500 ÷ 9,549 = 78.5 kW.","Both give the same power. The diesel has double the torque at half the speed, so it feels strong at low speed. The petrol engine must be revved higher to reach the same power."]},
      {"t":"p","text":"An engine's torque rises to a maximum at a medium speed and then falls, because the cylinders cannot fill so well at high speed. Power keeps rising until the torque falls fast enough to outweigh the speed. The torque curve shows how flexible an engine is."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Compression ratio and efficiency"},
      {"t":"p","text":"A higher compression ratio squeezes the mixture harder, extracts more work from each unit of fuel and raises efficiency. The limit in a petrol engine is **knock**, or detonation, in which the last part of the mixture ignites on its own before the flame reaches it. Knock causes a sharp pinging noise and, if it continues, melts pistons and damages bearings. Fuel with a higher **octane number** resists knock, so high-compression engines need higher-octane fuel. Diesel fuel needs the opposite, easy self-ignition, measured by its **cetane number**."},
      {"t":"example","title":"Compression ratio","steps":["A cylinder has a swept volume of 450 cm³ and a clearance volume of 45 cm³.","Total volume = 450 + 45 = 495 cm³.","Compression ratio = 495 ÷ 45 = 11 to 1.","If a milled cylinder head removes 5 cm³ from the clearance volume, the clearance is 40 cm³, and the ratio becomes 490 ÷ 40 = 12.25 to 1. The engine may now knock on the fuel it ran on before."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Where the energy goes"},
      {"t":"table","head":["Share of the fuel energy","Where it goes (typical petrol engine)"],"rows":[["About 30 to 35%","Useful work at the crankshaft"],["About 30 to 35%","Hot exhaust gas"],["About 25 to 30%","Coolant and radiator"],["About 5 to 10%","Friction, pumping and heat from the engine surface"]],"caption":"Diesel engines convert a larger share to work. Figures are approximate."},
      {"t":"example","title":"Fuel used by an engine","steps":["An engine produces 60 kW with an efficiency of 30%, so it burns fuel at 60 ÷ 0.30 = 200 kW of energy.","Petrol holds about 9.4 kWh of energy in each litre.","Fuel used per hour = 200 ÷ 9.4 = 21.3 litres per hour at full power.","At a part-load of 15 kW the same engine runs less efficiently, perhaps 20%, so 15 ÷ 0.20 = 75 kW of fuel energy, or 8.0 litres per hour."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"What limits an engine"},
      {"t":"ul","items":["**Breathing:** the volume of air the engine can draw in. Compare the **volumetric efficiency**, the amount drawn in against the swept volume.","**Friction:** piston rings, bearings and valve gear absorb part of the power. Thin low-friction oils help.","**Heat:** the metal can only take so much, so cooling and the strength of the materials limit pressure and speed.","**Knock:** limits compression ratio and boost pressure for the fuel in use."]},
      {"t":"note","kind":"key","text":"An engine that has lost compression, breathing or fuel supply loses torque first. If the customer says it feels weak, think about air, fuel, spark and compression in that order."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"tip","text":"When you read a specification, note the speed at which torque or power is given. 200 N·m at 1,500 rpm and 200 N·m at 4,500 rpm are very different engines."}
    ]},
    {"id": "m04-l4", "title": "Engine block and its parts", "minutes": 5, "body": [
      {"t":"p","text":"The engine is built from a few large castings and many moving parts. This lesson names the main parts and explains what each one does."},
      {"t":"diagram","name":"engine-cross-section"},
      {"t":"h","text":"Block, liners and head"},
      {"t":"p","text":"The **cylinder block** is the main casting. It holds the cylinders, the crankshaft bearings and the passages for oil and coolant. It is made from cast iron or aluminium alloy. Some blocks have separate **cylinder liners** pressed in, either dry (not touching coolant) or wet (surrounded by coolant). The **cylinder head** bolts on top of the block and contains the valves, ports and combustion chambers. The **head gasket** seals the joint between block and head, keeping combustion gas, oil and coolant in their own passages."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Materials and manufacture"},
      {"t":"p","text":"**Cast iron** is strong, cheap, wears well and damps vibration, but it is heavy. **Aluminium alloy** is much lighter and carries heat away quickly, but it is softer and needs hard liners or special coatings in the bores. Many modern engines use aluminium blocks with cast-iron liners or coated bores, and aluminium heads. Aluminium expands more than iron when it heats up, so a bolted joint between the two needs a good gasket, careful tightening and no overheating. Overheating warps an aluminium head far more easily than an iron one."},
      {"t":"p","text":"Inside the block are **galleries**, drilled passages that carry oil under pressure to bearings, and **water jackets**, the spaces around the cylinders that carry coolant. A blocked gallery starves a bearing. A cracked water jacket leaks coolant into the oil or the cylinder. **Freeze plugs** (core plugs) are metal discs that close the holes left by casting. A leaking plug is a coolant leak."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Piston assembly"},
      {"t":"ul","items":["**Piston:** moves up and down in the cylinder and takes the force of combustion. It is usually made of aluminium alloy.","**Piston rings:** the top ring seals the combustion gas. The second ring helps seal and scrape oil. The oil control ring scrapes surplus oil off the cylinder wall.","**Gudgeon pin (piston pin):** connects the piston to the small end of the connecting rod.","**Connecting rod:** links the piston to the crankshaft. It must be strong and light."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Crankshaft and bearings"},
      {"t":"p","text":"The **crankshaft** is a forged or cast shaft with offset **crankpins** that turn the up-and-down motion into rotation. It rotates on **main bearings** in the block, and each connecting rod turns on a **big-end bearing**. These plain bearings are thin shells with an oil film between the shell and shaft. They carry the load on the oil, not on metal. Counterweights balance the crankshaft."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Other main parts"},
      {"t":"ul","items":["**Flywheel:** stores energy and smooths the power pulses. It carries the starter ring gear.","**Sump (oil pan):** bolted under the block, it holds the engine oil.","**Harmonic balancer (damper):** on the front of the crankshaft, it reduces torsional vibration.","**Crankshaft seals:** front and rear seals stop oil escaping where the shaft leaves the block."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Bearings in detail"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Crankshaft bearings are made as pairs of thin **half shells** with layers of steel, a soft bearing metal and a thin overlay. One half has an oil hole or groove, and each has a locating tab that fits a notch in the housing so it cannot spin. Shells are slightly oversize in the housing so that tightening **crushes** them firmly in place and ensures good heat transfer."},
      {"t":"p","text":"A **thrust bearing** or thrust washer controls the end float, the movement of the crankshaft along its length. Dirt, a lack of oil or a bent shaft marks the shells. A shell that has a bright patch, a scratched surface, copper showing or flaking is worn and needs replacing, and the cause must be found."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"key","text":"Plain bearings run on a film of oil. Dirty oil, low oil pressure or a long period without oil removes the film, and the bearing and shaft wear very quickly."}
    ]},
    {"id": "m04-l5", "title": "Pistons, rings, rods and cylinder wear in detail", "minutes": 5, "body": [
      {"t":"p","text":"The piston assembly works in the harshest place in the engine. It moves up and down thousands of times a minute, takes the force of combustion, seals the cylinder and does it in a film of oil. This lesson looks at how it is built, how it wears and how to check it. A good understanding here lets you diagnose oil consumption, blow-by and loss of compression."},
      {"t":"h","text":"The piston"},
      {"t":"p","text":"A piston has a **crown** (the top, shaped to suit the combustion chamber), ring grooves, and a **skirt** that guides it in the bore. It is made of aluminium alloy so it is light and sheds heat, but aluminium expands more than the iron or aluminium cylinder around it. The piston is therefore made slightly oval and smaller in diameter at the top, and fitted with a small **piston-to-wall clearance**. If the clearance is too small the piston can seize when hot, and if it is too large the piston rocks and makes a slapping noise when the engine is cold."},
      {"t":"p","text":"Some engines squirt oil under the crown through **piston cooling jets**. A blocked jet can burn a piston."},
      {"t":"p","text":"Many pistons are marked with an arrow or notch that must face the front of the engine, because the gudgeon pin is slightly offset and the skirt shape is not symmetrical. Fit them as the manual shows."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Piston rings"},
      {"t":"table","head":["Ring","Job","Shape and notes"],"rows":[["Top compression ring","Seals combustion gas in the cylinder","Barrel-faced or tapered. Often coated for wear resistance."],["Second ring","Seals and scrapes oil down","Tapered or with a scraper edge. Fit it the right way up."],["Oil control ring","Scrapes surplus oil off the wall and returns it to the sump","Three pieces, two thin steel rails and an expander, or a single piece"]]},
      {"t":"p","text":"Each ring has a small **end gap** so it can expand when hot. If the gap is too small, the ends touch, the ring bows against the wall and it scuffs the bore. If it is too large, gas leaks past. To check, push the ring squarely into the cylinder with a piston, so it lies flat in the unworn part of the bore, and measure the gap with a feeler gauge. Check the **side clearance** in the groove as well. When you fit the rings, put the marking (such as TOP) uppermost, and space the end gaps evenly around the piston, never in line with each other or with the gudgeon pin."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Connecting rod and big end"},
      {"t":"p","text":"The connecting rod is a forged steel or alloy link. Its **small end** carries the gudgeon pin and its **big end** wraps round the crankpin in a two-part bearing. The rod cap and rod are machined together and must be refitted the same way round, so they are numbered or marked. The rod bolts take huge forces and are often **torque-to-yield**, to be replaced every time. A rod bearing that has been starved of oil makes a deep knocking noise that gets louder under load, and the engine may throw a rod through the block if it is ignored."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Cylinder wear"},
      {"t":"p","text":"The cylinder wall is worn by rings, dirt and the oil film failing at the top of the ring travel. The top of the bore wears most, so a worn bore is wider at the top (taper). The ring pressure and the rocking of the piston can also leave it slightly oval. A worn bore lets combustion gas past the rings, called **blow-by**, which shows as oil mist from the breather and weak compression. It also lets oil into the cylinder, which burns and gives blue smoke and oil consumption."},
      {"t":"table","head":["Symptom","Likely cause"],"rows":[["Blue smoke, oil loss, good compression","Worn valve stem seals or guides. Smoke on start-up is typical."],["Blue smoke on acceleration, falling compression","Worn rings and bores"],["Heavy blow-by and low compression in one cylinder","Broken or stuck ring, burnt piston"],["Ticking that disappears when hot","Loose piston in a worn bore (piston slap)"],["White smoke and loss of coolant","Head gasket or crack, coolant entering the cylinder"]]},
      {"t":"p","text":"If a bore has a shiny, **glazed** surface from long, light-load running, the rings cannot bed in. A light **honing** gives a fine cross-hatch pattern at about 45° to hold oil. A worn bore beyond the limits must be rebored to a larger size with an oversize piston, or the block replaced or sleeved."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"safety","text":"Always wear eye protection and gloves when you handle sharp ring ends and cast parts, and never use compressed air to clean parts or hone dust off your skin."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"key","text":"Blue smoke means oil is burning, and where it burns tells you the cause. Smoke at start-up points to the valve stem seals. Smoke under load points to the rings."}
    ]},
    {"id": "m04-l6", "title": "The valvetrain", "minutes": 5, "body": [
      {"t":"p","text":"The valvetrain controls the opening and closing of the valves. It is driven by the crankshaft through the timing drive. This lesson explains the parts and why their clearances and timing matter."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Valves and springs"},
      {"t":"p","text":"The **intake valve** lets air or mixture in. The **exhaust valve** lets burnt gas out and runs much hotter, so it is made of tougher material. Each valve has a head, a stem and a face that seals against a **valve seat** in the head. A **valve spring** closes the valve and keeps it closed. **Valve guides** keep the stem straight, and **valve stem seals** stop oil running down the stem into the cylinder."},
      {"t":"diagram","name":"valvetrain"},
      {"t":"h","text":"Camshaft and followers"},
      {"t":"p","text":"A **camshaft** has lobes, which are shaped bumps, that push open the valves as it turns. Each lobe pushes through a follower, a **tappet** or **cam follower** that rides on the lobe. In an **overhead camshaft (OHC)** engine the camshaft sits in the head and acts on the valves directly or through **rocker arms**. In a **pushrod (OHV)** engine the camshaft sits in the block and moves the valves through lifters, pushrods and rockers."},
      {"t":"table","head":["Valve gear","Layout","Notes"],"rows":[["Overhead camshaft (OHC)","One camshaft in the head, acting on the valves through followers or rockers","Common. Simple and light."],["Double overhead camshaft (DOHC)","Two camshafts, one for intake and one for exhaust, often four valves per cylinder","Better breathing at high speed"],["Overhead valve (OHV, pushrod)","The camshaft is in the block and pushes the valves through lifters, pushrods and rockers","Compact and strong. Used on some V engines and trucks."]]},
      {"t":"p","text":"The cam has a round part, the **base circle**, and a raised part, the **lobe**. The height of the lobe above the base circle is the **lift**, and the number of crankshaft degrees the valve is open is its **duration**. A lobe with more lift and duration lets in more air and gives more power at high speed, but may idle roughly."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Valve clearance"},
      {"t":"p","text":"When an engine warms up, the parts expand, so a small gap, called **valve clearance**, is needed to be sure the valve can close fully. Too small a clearance stops the valve closing, causing burnt valves and loss of compression. Too large makes a tapping noise and reduces how far the valve opens. Measure with a feeler gauge, usually when the engine is cold and the valve is on the base circle of the cam, and follow the manual. Many engines use **hydraulic tappets** that adjust themselves with oil pressure, so need no regular adjustment."},
      {"t":"example","title":"Checking valve clearance","steps":["Remove the cover and turn the engine to the position in the manual for the first valve.","The specification is 0.20 mm. Slide the 0.20 mm blade between cam and follower.","It should slide with a light drag. If a 0.25 mm blade also fits, the clearance is too big.","Adjust and recheck as the manual describes."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Variable valve timing"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Many engines have **variable valve timing**, which changes the camshaft's position relative to the crankshaft while the engine runs, so the engine gives good power at high speed and economy at low speed. It is controlled by the engine computer and oil pressure, so low or dirty oil can cause faults."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Adjusting clearance with shims"},
      {"t":"p","text":"On many engines with bucket tappets the clearance is set by a small steel **shim** placed in or over the bucket. To change the clearance, you replace the shim with one of a different thickness. A thicker shim reduces the clearance. A thinner one increases it. Measure each clearance and write it down before you remove anything."},
      {"t":"example","title":"Choosing a new shim","steps":["The specification for an intake valve is 0.20 mm. You measure 0.32 mm, which is too large by 0.12 mm.","The old shim is 3.20 mm thick. To close the gap by 0.12 mm you need a shim 0.12 mm thicker: 3.20 + 0.12 = 3.32 mm.","For an exhaust valve with a specification of 0.30 mm, you measure 0.22 mm, which is too small by 0.08 mm. The old shim is 3.40 mm, so you need one 0.08 mm thinner: 3.40 − 0.08 = 3.32 mm.","Fit the new shim, turn the engine two full turns by hand and measure again to confirm."]},
      {"t":"note","kind":"tip","text":"A tapping noise from the top of the engine that fades as the engine warms up can be low oil pressure, a faulty hydraulic tappet or excessive valve clearance. Check the oil level and condition first."}
    ]},
    {"id": "m04-l7", "title": "Cylinder head and valve service", "minutes": 5, "body": [
      {"t":"p","text":"When an engine loses compression, overheats or burns oil through its valve gear, the cylinder head usually comes off. This lesson explains how a head is removed, inspected, reconditioned and refitted. Head work is common and teaches the habits of clean, careful engine repair."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Removing the head"},
      {"t":"ol","items":["Let the engine cool completely and drain the coolant. Disconnect the battery.","Take off the intake and exhaust connections, the timing cover and the timing belt or chain, noting the timing marks first.","Remove the cam cover, then the camshafts and followers, keeping each follower with its valve so that it can be refitted in the same place.","Loosen the head bolts a little at a time in the reverse of the tightening order, working from the outside inwards. Pulling them in order stops the head from warping.","Lift the head off. Do not pry between the head and block, because it damages the faces. Use the lifting points or tap with a soft mallet."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Inspecting the head"},
      {"t":"ul","items":["Clean the faces carefully, taking care not to scratch aluminium or let debris fall into the cylinders or oil passages.","Check for **cracks**, especially between the valve seats and around the spark plug or injector hole. Cracks can be found by pressure testing, dye or by inspection.","Check **flatness** with a straight edge and feeler gauge in several directions, along the length, across the width and on both diagonals. Compare the gap with the limit in the manual. A warped head can be machined (skimmed) only within the maker's limits.","Check the **valve guides** for wear by measuring the clearance between stem and guide, or by rocking the valve in the guide with a dial gauge.","Inspect the **valve seats** and **valve faces** for pitting, burning and an uneven seating band.","Check each **valve spring** for free length, squareness and pressure, and compare with the manual."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Reconditioning the valves"},
      {"t":"p","text":"If the seating band is damaged, the valve and its seat are refaced. The seats are cut at the correct angles, often 45° with narrow top and throat cuts, and the valve face is ground to match. The valve is then **lapped** by turning it on the seat with fine grinding paste until it forms a continuous grey ring. Clean away all of the paste. Check the seal by pouring a little solvent into the port with the valve closed. The solvent should not leak past the seat. New valve stem seals are fitted on every rebuild, as old ones harden."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Refitting the head"},
      {"t":"ol","items":["Clean the block deck and the head face. Use a bolt or tap to clean the bolt holes, and make sure that there is no oil or coolant in blind holes. Trapped liquid can crack the block when the bolt is tightened.","Fit a new head gasket the right way up, without sealant unless the manual calls for it. A multi-layer steel gasket must be fitted dry and clean.","Lower the head squarely onto the dowels and fit new head bolts if the manual calls for stretch bolts.","Tighten the bolts in the specified sequence, usually in a spiral from the centre outwards, in stages: a first torque, a second torque, and then a quarter or half turn by angle as stated.","Refit the cam gear and timing drive, check the timing marks and turn the engine by hand through two turns.","Refill with coolant, bleed air from the system, and check for leaks, overheating and warning lights on the first run."]},
      {"t":"example","title":"Reading a head bolt specification","steps":["The manual says: stage 1, 30 N·m; stage 2, 60 N·m; stage 3, 90° turn; stage 4, 90° turn. The bolts must be replaced after use.","Work in the stated order. Tighten every bolt to 30 N·m, then every bolt to 60 N·m.","Then turn each bolt through a further 90°, in order, using an angle gauge, and repeat for the fourth stage. The total is a half turn past 60 N·m.","Do not mix the stages or guess the angle. A bolt loosened by half a turn must be fully tightened again as if new."]},
      {"t":"note","kind":"safety","text":"Never reuse stretch (torque-to-yield) head bolts, and never tighten a head by feel. A head that lifts causes gasket failure, overheating and damage to the bores."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"tip","text":"Photograph the engine before you strip it, and put each valve, follower and spring in a labelled tray. Parts that have worn together must go back in the same place."}
    ]},
    {"id": "m04-l8", "title": "Timing drives", "minutes": 5, "body": [
      {"t":"p","text":"The **timing drive** links the crankshaft to the camshaft so the valves open at exactly the right moment. Getting it wrong can destroy an engine, so this lesson explains it carefully."},
      {"t":"diagram","name":"timing-belt-drive"},
      {"t":"h","text":"Belts and chains"},
      {"t":"p","text":"A **timing belt** is a reinforced rubber belt with teeth. It is quiet, light and cheap, but it wears and must be replaced at the interval in the manual, usually by distance or years. A **timing chain** is a metal chain that is built to last, but it can stretch and become noisy, and it needs good oil. Some engines use gears instead. A **tensioner** keeps the correct tension, and **idler pulleys** guide the belt. These parts are usually replaced together with the belt as a kit."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Timing marks"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Marks on the crankshaft pulley, camshaft sprockets and engine casing show the correct position. They must line up before the belt is removed and when it is refitted. If the engine has no marks, the manual shows how to lock the shafts with special tools. Never turn the crankshaft or camshaft while the belt is off unless the manual allows it."},
      {"t":"p","text":"The camshaft turns at half the speed of the crankshaft, so its sprocket has twice as many teeth. A crankshaft sprocket with 20 teeth drives a camshaft sprocket with 40 teeth. A **double overhead cam** engine has two camshafts driven from the crankshaft, or one driving the other by a short chain or gears."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Interference engines"},
      {"t":"p","text":"On an **interference engine** the open valves and the piston can occupy the same space at different times. If the timing belt breaks while the engine is running, the camshaft stops but the pistons keep moving, and the pistons can hit the valves and bend them. A **non-interference engine** has enough clearance for this not to happen. Check the workshop manual to know which type you are dealing with, and never exceed the replacement interval."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Other parts driven by the belt"},
      {"t":"p","text":"On many engines the timing belt also drives the **water pump**. If the pump fails and seizes, it can break the belt. It makes sense to replace the water pump when the belt is replaced, because most of the labour is the same."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Replacing a timing belt"},
      {"t":"ol","items":["Disconnect the battery. Remove the drive belts, covers and anything in the way, taking notes of the routing.","Turn the engine by hand, in the normal direction of rotation, to the timing position for cylinder 1, and check that every mark lines up.","Lock the camshafts and crankshaft with the timing tools if the manual calls for them.","Release the tensioner and take off the old belt. Replace the tensioner, idlers and water pump if the kit calls for it.","Fit the new belt, starting at the crankshaft and keeping the belt tight on the side without the tensioner, and ending at the tensioner.","Set the tension as the manual states, remove the locking tools and turn the engine by hand through two full turns. Check the marks again.","Refit covers, torque the crankshaft bolt, refit the drive belts and start the engine. Listen and check for warning lights."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Timing chains and variable cam phasers"},
      {"t":"p","text":"A chain stretches slowly and its guides wear. The signs are a rattle on a cold start, slow timing, and a fault code reporting that the camshaft and crankshaft signals do not match. Many engines also have a **cam phaser** on the camshaft, a hydraulic device that turns the cam a little relative to its sprocket, so the timing can change with speed and load. A phaser depends on clean oil at the right pressure. A rattle at start-up and codes for camshaft position can mean a phaser or chain fault."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"safety","text":"A timing belt that is oily, frayed, cracked or missing teeth is a red item on any inspection. Do not start the engine after replacing a belt until you have turned it by hand through two full turns and checked that the timing marks line up again."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"tip","text":"Photograph the timing marks and the belt routing before you take anything off. It costs seconds and can save the engine."}
    ]},
    {"id": "m04-l9", "title": "Engine balance, vibration and engine mountings", "minutes": 5, "body": [
      {"t":"p","text":"An engine is a set of heavy parts moving up and down and spinning at high speed. If nothing was done to control them, it would shake itself and the vehicle to pieces. This lesson explains how engines are balanced, why firing order and cylinder count matter, how flywheels and dampers reduce vibration and how engine mounts isolate the rest of the vehicle from what remains."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Firing intervals"},
      {"t":"p","text":"In a four-stroke engine each cylinder fires once every 720° of crankshaft rotation. The firing interval between cylinders is 720° divided by the number of cylinders. An even interval gives smooth running."},
      {"t":"table","head":["Cylinders","Firing interval","Typical firing order","Notes"],"rows":[["3 inline","240°","1-2-3","Needs a balance shaft for smoothness"],["4 inline","180°","1-3-4-2","Good primary balance. Vibrates at twice engine speed."],["6 inline","120°","1-5-3-6-2-4","Very smooth, naturally balanced"],["8 in a V","90°","Varies by engine","Smooth when the crankshaft is built to suit"]],"caption":"Check the manual for the numbering and firing order of the engine you are working on."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Balancing the moving parts"},
      {"t":"p","text":"The pistons and rods are matched in weight, and the crankshaft has **counterweights** that cancel the force of the rising and falling pistons. A four-cylinder inline engine still has a vibration at twice the crankshaft speed, called secondary imbalance, which grows with engine size. Larger four-cylinder engines therefore often have a pair of **balance shafts** that turn in opposite directions at twice engine speed to cancel it. A balance-shaft chain that has been fitted out of time causes a harsh vibration."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Torsional vibration, dampers and the flywheel"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Each power pulse twists the crankshaft slightly, and the shaft springs back. This is torsional vibration, and it can reach the point where the shaft breaks. A **harmonic balancer** at the front is a rubber-bonded ring that absorbs it. A rubber that perishes allows the ring to slip, which shows as a wobble or cracks in the rubber. The **flywheel** is a heavy disc that smooths the pulses between power strokes. Many diesel and some petrol engines use a **dual-mass flywheel**, two discs joined by springs that soak up torsional vibration before it reaches the gearbox. A worn dual-mass flywheel rattles at idle and makes a shudder when the clutch is engaged."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Engine mountings"},
      {"t":"p","text":"The engine is mounted on the vehicle by rubber or fluid-filled **mounts**. They hold the engine's weight, resist its torque reaction (the engine tries to twist as it drives the wheels) and absorb vibration so that it does not reach the body."},
      {"t":"table","head":["Type","How it works","Typical failure"],"rows":[["Rubber","A block of rubber bonded between metal plates","Cracks, splits and sags"],["Hydraulic","Rubber chamber filled with fluid that damps movement","Leaks fluid, and gives a clunk or loss of damping"],["Active or electronic","Controls its stiffness through a solenoid or vacuum","Sticks or loses control, giving idle vibration"]]},
      {"t":"ul","items":["**Symptoms of a bad mount:** vibration at idle that eases when the engine speed rises, clunks on accelerating or lifting off, the engine seen to move a lot when the throttle is blipped, and shifting noises.","**Checking:** with an assistant, watch the engine as the gearbox is put into drive and reverse with the brake held. A mount that lets the engine lift or shift is worn. Look for splits, separated rubber and leaking fluid.","**Effects of a failed mount:** extra strain on pipes, hoses, wiring, the exhaust and the driveshafts, so failure causes other damage."]},
      {"t":"note","kind":"key","text":"Vibration has a cause. Link it to engine speed, vehicle speed, load and temperature, and it will point to the part: a misfire, a balancer, a mount, a wheel, or a driveshaft."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"safety","text":"Support the engine with a hoist or a stand before you remove a mount. Never trust one mount to hold the weight of the engine while you work."}
    ]},
    {"id": "m04-l10", "title": "Dismantling and inspection", "minutes": 5, "body": [
      {"t":"p","text":"Removing an engine or stripping it down is skilled work. This lesson covers the habits that keep parts in order, and the main checks that tell you whether they can be used again."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Preparation and removal"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Disconnect the battery and drain the fluids into containers. Label every connector, hose and wire, take photographs and bag the small parts with their fasteners. Support the engine with a hoist before removing the mounts, check the hoist rating and keep clear of the load. Follow the manual's removal order, because some engines come out with the gearbox and others come out alone."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Dismantling"},
      {"t":"ul","items":["Loosen head bolts in the reverse of the tightening sequence, gradually, to avoid distorting the head.","Keep parts that wear together in order: pistons with their cylinders, bearing shells with their caps, valves in their positions.","Never use a screwdriver to pry gasket faces apart if the manual shows a tap-off method, because scratches cause leaks.","Cover openings to keep dirt out."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Cleaning and inspecting"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Clean parts so you can see cracks, scoring and wear. Do not wash bearings in solvent. Check:"},
      {"t":"ul","items":["**Bores:** measure with a bore gauge at the top, middle and bottom, and across and along the engine, to find taper and out-of-round.","**Crank journals:** measure with a micrometer at several points. Compare with the manual's sizes and limits.","**Cylinder head:** check the flatness with a straightedge and feeler gauge, and look for cracks near valve seats and between ports.","**Valves and springs:** check for pitting, bending and spring height."]},
      {"t":"table","head":["Part","Tool","Look for"],"rows":[["Cylinder bore","Bore gauge or inside micrometer","Taper, out-of-round, scoring"],["Crankshaft journal","Outside micrometer","Wear, ovality, scoring"],["Cylinder head face","Straightedge and feeler gauge","Warping, cracks"],["Valve spring","Rule or spring tester","Short free length, bending"]],"caption":"Inspection checks"},
      {"t":"h","text":"Gaskets, sealant and reassembly"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Clean all gasket faces without scratching. Use new gaskets and seals, and use sealant only where the manual says. Too much sealant can squeeze into oil passages and block them. Oil the bearings and bores when assembling, tighten to the specified torque and sequence, and turn the engine by hand to check it turns freely before you start it."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Measuring bearing clearance with Plastigauge"},
      {"t":"ol","items":["Wipe the journal and shell clean and dry. Do not oil them.","Lay a short piece of Plastigauge along the journal, away from the oil hole.","Fit the cap and tighten it to the full torque, without turning the shaft.","Remove the cap and compare the width of the flattened strip with the printed scale on the packet. The scale gives the clearance in millimetres.","Compare the result with the manual. If it is too large, fit thicker shells or regrind the shaft. If it is too small, check for dirt or the wrong shells."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Rebuild or replace?"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Sometimes the cost of repairing an engine is more than a reconditioned replacement. The decision depends on the extent of the damage, the cost and availability of parts and machining, the age and value of the vehicle, and the warranty. A **short engine** is a block with crankshaft and pistons. A **long engine** adds the cylinder head. A **reconditioned engine** has been rebuilt to a standard and has a warranty. Explain the options to the customer in plain language and give a written estimate for each."},
      {"t":"h","text":"First start after a rebuild"},
      {"t":"ul","items":["Pre-fill the filter and prime the oil system, so the bearings do not run dry on first start.","Check that every fluid is filled, all hoses and connectors are secure and the timing marks are right.","Start the engine and watch the oil pressure light. Stop at once if it stays on.","Run at a moderate speed and follow the maker's running-in advice. Check for leaks and noises and recheck the oil level and coolant level after the first run."]},
      {"t":"note","kind":"key","text":"Cleanliness is the most important part of engine assembly. A single grain of grit in a bearing can ruin a crankshaft."}
    ]},
    {"id": "m04-l11", "title": "Modern engine technology", "minutes": 5, "body": [
      {"t":"p","text":"Rising fuel prices and stricter emission limits have changed the engine. A technician today meets turbochargers, direct injection, cylinder deactivation, stop-start systems and hybrid cycles on ordinary cars. You need to know what each one does and how it changes servicing and diagnosis. This lesson is an overview, and later modules study the fuel, boost and electronic systems in detail."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Downsizing and forced induction"},
      {"t":"p","text":"**Downsizing** means using a smaller engine, often with a turbocharger, to give the power of a larger one while using less fuel when driven gently. A turbocharger uses exhaust gas to spin a compressor and push extra air into the cylinders. More air means more fuel can be burned, so power increases. The engine runs at a higher pressure and temperature, which makes oil quality, cooling and correct maintenance more critical. Module 7 covers the turbocharger."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Direct injection"},
      {"t":"p","text":"In a **direct injection** petrol engine, fuel is sprayed straight into the cylinder at high pressure instead of into the intake port. This allows precise control, cooling of the charge and a higher compression ratio. Because fuel no longer washes over the back of the intake valves, carbon can build up there over time, which causes rough running and misfires. Direct injection engines may also need a particulate filter. Follow the schedule for fuel and oil quality."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Variable valve timing and lift"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Systems that change the valve timing, and in some engines the lift, let the engine breathe in the best way at every speed. They improve torque, fuel economy and emissions. They depend on oil pressure and clean oil, and respond to the engine control unit. Faults appear as poor idle, loss of power, rattles at start and codes for camshaft position."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Cylinder deactivation"},
      {"t":"p","text":"At light load some engines shut off half the cylinders by keeping their valves closed, which cuts the pumping losses and saves fuel. The system is silent when it works. If it fails, expect a rough engine, odd sounds and a warning light. It relies on special lifters and correct oil."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Stop-start"},
      {"t":"p","text":"A stop-start system switches the engine off when the vehicle stops, and restarts it when the driver moves. It saves fuel in heavy traffic. The engine is fitted with a stronger starter, a battery designed for repeated cycles (an enhanced flooded or absorbed glass mat type) and extra controls. Fitting an ordinary battery shortens its life and can disable the system. Always use the specified type and register a new battery with the control unit if the manual requires it."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Hybrid-friendly cycles"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Hybrid vehicles often use an engine that runs the **Atkinson** or **Miller** cycle, in which the intake valve closes late so that the engine's expansion stroke is longer than its compression stroke. This improves efficiency but gives less power per litre, which the electric motor makes up. The engine may stop and start many times without the driver noticing, so never assume that a silent hybrid engine is off. Module 13 covers hybrid vehicles."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Materials and long-life features"},
      {"t":"ul","items":["Aluminium blocks with coated bores and plastic or composite parts reduce weight.","Low-friction coatings, thin piston rings and low-viscosity oils reduce friction.","Variable-capacity oil pumps and split cooling systems reduce the energy the engine uses to run itself.","Extended service intervals rely on high-quality synthetic oil and on correct oil level and specification."]},
      {"t":"note","kind":"key","text":"New technology does not change the basics. Every engine still needs air, fuel, spark or compression ignition, and good oil and coolant. When a modern engine misbehaves, test the basics first."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"tip","text":"Always check for technical information about the specific engine before you start. A small difference, such as the oil specification or a special bleeding procedure, can matter more than any general rule."}
    ]}
  ],
  "practical": {"title":"Identify engine parts and measure wear","objective":"Identify the main parts of a cut-away or stripped engine, then measure a cylinder bore and crankshaft journals and compare the readings with the specification.","time":"90 minutes","tools":["A cut-away or stripped training engine on a stand","Bore gauge or inside micrometer and outside micrometer","Straightedge and feeler gauge set","Workshop manual or specification sheet","Labels, a marker and a notepad"],"safety":["Keep fingers clear of pinch points when you turn the crankshaft.","Wear safety glasses and gloves, and handle sharp edges with care.","Lift heavy parts with a hoist or with help, and do not carry a cylinder block alone.","Keep the area tidy and wipe up oil to avoid slips.","Do not use a micrometer as a hammer."],"steps":["Name each major part of the engine, writing its name and function on a label.","Identify the engine layout, cylinder numbering and firing order from the manual.","Turn the crankshaft by hand and watch the valves, noting which are open in each stroke.","Calibrate the bore gauge or micrometer and check that the zero is correct.","Measure the cylinder bore at the top, middle and bottom, across and along the engine.","Calculate taper and out-of-round from your readings.","Measure the crankshaft main journals at two positions each, 90° apart.","Check the cylinder head face for flatness using a straightedge and feeler gauge.","Compare every measurement with the manual and mark each as within limit or not.","Write a short note on what the readings tell you about the engine's condition.","Clean and put away the tools and return the engine parts to their trays."],"checks":["I can name the main engine parts and say what each one does.","I can describe the four strokes and the valve positions in each.","I measured a bore and calculated taper and out-of-round.","I measured journals with a micrometer and compared them with the specification.","I checked a cylinder head for flatness and recorded the result."]},
  "quiz": [
    {"id":"m04-q01","q":"What does the crankshaft do in an engine?","options":["It converts the up-and-down motion of the pistons into rotation","It stores fuel and feeds it to each cylinder in turn at high pressure","It opens and closes the valves at the correct moment in the cycle","It cools the engine by moving air across the cylinder head surface"],"answer":0,"level":1,"lesson":"m04-l1","explain":"Connecting rods push on offset crankpins, which turns the crankshaft."},
    {"id":"m04-q02","q":"How does a diesel engine ignite its fuel?","options":["A glowing wire in the exhaust manifold lights the fuel as it passes in","The starter motor heats the fuel in the injectors before it is sprayed","Air is compressed until it is so hot that injected fuel ignites by itself","A spark plug in each cylinder ignites the mixture at exactly the right moment"],"answer":2,"level":1,"lesson":"m04-l1","explain":"Diesel engines have no spark plugs. High compression heats the air enough to ignite the fuel."},
    {"id":"m04-q03","q":"What is the purpose of the firing order?","options":["To balance the engine and spread the power pulses evenly","To make the engine run in the reverse direction when needed","To tell the starter motor how many turns it must make to start","To decide which cylinder gets the most fuel in each cycle"],"answer":0,"level":2,"lesson":"m04-l1","explain":"A good firing order smooths the engine and reduces vibration. Always check the manual for the correct order."},
    {"id":"m04-q04","q":"A cylinder has a bore of 8.0 cm and a stroke of 8.0 cm. What is its swept volume to the nearest cm³?","options":["64 cm³","201 cm³","804 cm³","402 cm³"],"answer":3,"level":2,"lesson":"m04-l1","explain":"π ÷ 4 × 8² × 8 = 402 cm³. Multiply by the number of cylinders for the whole engine."},
    {"id":"m04-q05","q":"What is the main advantage of a V engine layout?","options":["It has fewer moving parts than an inline engine of the same power","It is shorter than an inline engine with the same number of cylinders","It can run on both petrol and diesel without any changes","It needs no cooling system because of the angle between rows"],"answer":1,"level":1,"lesson":"m04-l1","explain":"Two rows of cylinders at an angle make the engine shorter, though wider."},
    {"id":"m04-q06","q":"When is a rise in compression ratio a concern?","options":["It reduces the power from the same fuel but saves some oil","It puts more stress on the engine and can cause knock on poor fuel","It makes the engine run quietly and smoothly at all times","It lowers the temperature of the burning gas in the chamber"],"answer":1,"level":3,"lesson":"m04-l1","explain":"Higher compression raises power and efficiency, but risks knock if the fuel is not suited."},
    {"id":"m04-q07","q":"What is the stroke of an engine?","options":["The width of the cylinder measured across its diameter","The number of times the crankshaft turns in one minute","The volume of fuel drawn into the cylinder every cycle","The distance the piston travels from TDC to BDC"],"answer":3,"level":1,"lesson":"m04-l1","explain":"Stroke is the piston's travel between top and bottom dead centre. Bore is the cylinder diameter and engine speed is measured in rpm."},
    {"id":"m04-q08","q":"What does an engine code stamped on the block help you to do?","options":["Work out the year that the owner bought the vehicle","Know how many kilometres the engine has already covered","Find the correct parts and data for that engine","Decide how often the oil should be topped up each week"],"answer":2,"level":2,"lesson":"m04-l1","explain":"Many models have several engines. The code identifies which one is fitted, so you can find the right parts, torque values and clearances."},
    {"id":"m04-q09","q":"How many crankshaft revolutions are needed for one complete four-stroke cycle?","options":["Half a revolution","Two","One","Four"],"answer":1,"level":1,"lesson":"m04-l2","explain":"Four strokes take two turns, which is 720°. Each cylinder fires once every two turns."},
    {"id":"m04-q10","q":"What is the correct order of the four strokes?","options":["Power, intake, compression, exhaust","Intake, compression, power, exhaust","Compression, intake, exhaust, power","Intake, power, compression, exhaust"],"answer":1,"level":1,"lesson":"m04-l2","explain":"Suck, squeeze, bang, blow. The mixture must be taken in and squeezed before it burns."},
    {"id":"m04-q11","q":"Which stroke produces the work that turns the crankshaft?","options":["The compression stroke, when the mixture is squeezed into a small space","The exhaust stroke, when the burnt gas is pushed out of the cylinder","The intake stroke, when the piston draws fresh air into the cylinder","The power stroke, when the burning gas pushes the piston down"],"answer":3,"level":2,"lesson":"m04-l2","explain":"Only the power stroke delivers energy. The other three strokes use energy from the flywheel."},
    {"id":"m04-q12","q":"Why does the camshaft turn at half the speed of the crankshaft?","options":["The camshaft is smaller and so it turns more slowly","The camshaft is driven through the engine's flywheel","The valves must open twice as fast as the pistons move","Each valve opens only once per two crankshaft turns"],"answer":3,"level":2,"lesson":"m04-l2","explain":"Each valve opens once per cycle, and a cycle takes two crankshaft turns."},
    {"id":"m04-q13","q":"What is valve overlap?","options":["A fault where one valve will not close all the way on its seat","The distance that the valve stem moves up and down in its guide","A short period at the end of exhaust when both valves are open","The time when both valves are closed together during the power stroke"],"answer":2,"level":3,"lesson":"m04-l2","explain":"Overlap helps scavenge the cylinder. It is designed in, and wrong timing disturbs it."},
    {"id":"m04-q14","q":"Which stroke does the actual work of pushing the piston down?","options":["The power stroke","The intake stroke that follows the exhaust","The compression stroke that precedes the spark","The exhaust stroke that clears the cylinder"],"answer":0,"level":2,"lesson":"m04-l2","explain":"The burning gas expands and forces the piston down during the power stroke, and the other three strokes only prepare for it or clear away the gas."},
    {"id":"m04-q15","q":"Why is a diesel engine built heavier than a petrol engine of the same size?","options":["It always runs at a much higher number of revolutions","It runs a far higher compression ratio and pressure","It uses a larger flywheel to smooth every power pulse","It burns cheaper fuel that deposits ash on the walls"],"answer":1,"level":2,"lesson":"m04-l2","explain":"Compression ignition needs a ratio of about 15:1 to 22:1, so peak pressures are much higher. The block, head, pistons and bearings need extra strength."},
    {"id":"m04-q16","q":"An intake valve opens 10° before TDC and closes 40° after BDC. For how many degrees of crankshaft rotation is it open?","options":["180°","190°","230°","50°"],"answer":2,"level":3,"lesson":"m04-l2","explain":"Opening 10° early, plus the full 180° intake stroke, plus 40° after BDC: 10 + 180 + 40 = 230°. Valves stay open longer than the stroke to improve breathing."},
    {"id":"m04-q17","q":"An engine makes 150 N·m at 5,000 rpm. What is the power?","options":["About 750 kilowatts","About 78.5 kW","About 30 kilowatts","About 157 kilowatts"],"answer":1,"level":2,"lesson":"m04-l3","explain":"Power = torque × rpm ÷ 9549 = 150 × 5,000 ÷ 9,549 = 78.5 kW."},
    {"id":"m04-q18","q":"A cylinder has a swept volume of 450 cm³ and a clearance volume of 45 cm³. What is its compression ratio?","options":["11 to 1","10 to 1","9 to 1","450 to 1"],"answer":0,"level":2,"lesson":"m04-l3","explain":"Total volume 495 cm³ divided by clearance 45 cm³ gives 11 to 1."},
    {"id":"m04-q19","q":"Why can machining the head face raise the compression ratio and cause knock?","options":["It makes the head weigh less so the engine turns faster","It increases the swept volume of every cylinder in the block","It cools the combustion chamber so that the fuel burns slower","It reduces the clearance volume, so it squeezes more"],"answer":3,"level":3,"lesson":"m04-l3","explain":"A thinner head or thinner head gasket reduces the combustion chamber volume. The higher ratio raises pressure and temperature and can make the fuel detonate."},
    {"id":"m04-q20","q":"What does a higher octane number show about a petrol?","options":["It contains much more energy per litre than other fuel","It resists knock better","It ignites more easily under pressure in a diesel engine","It is cheaper to produce than other grades of petrol"],"answer":1,"level":1,"lesson":"m04-l3","explain":"Octane measures resistance to knock. High-compression engines need higher octane. Cetane measures how easily diesel ignites."},
    {"id":"m04-q21","q":"Roughly what share of the fuel energy in a petrol engine becomes useful work at the crankshaft?","options":["About 70 to 80%","About 90 to 95%","About 5 to 10%","About 30 to 35%"],"answer":3,"level":3,"lesson":"m04-l3","explain":"Most of the energy is lost as heat in the exhaust and coolant. Even a good petrol engine converts only about a third to work."},
    {"id":"m04-q22","q":"Which part seals the joint between the cylinder block and the cylinder head?","options":["The crankshaft seal","The piston ring","The sump gasket","The head gasket"],"answer":3,"level":1,"lesson":"m04-l4","explain":"The head gasket keeps combustion gas, oil and coolant in their own passages."},
    {"id":"m04-q23","q":"What does the top piston ring mainly do?","options":["It holds the gudgeon pin in place inside the piston itself","It scrapes oil from the cylinder wall back down into the sump","It seals the combustion gas in the cylinder above the piston","It cools the piston crown by touching the coolant in the block"],"answer":2,"level":2,"lesson":"m04-l4","explain":"The top ring seals compression. The oil ring controls the oil film on the wall."},
    {"id":"m04-q24","q":"What carries the load on a crankshaft main bearing?","options":["A thin film of oil between the shell and the journal","Direct contact between the steel shell and the journal","The coolant that surrounds the block","A layer of grease packed in at assembly"],"answer":0,"level":2,"lesson":"m04-l4","explain":"Plain bearings run on oil. Without oil pressure the film breaks down and parts wear very quickly."},
    {"id":"m04-q25","q":"What is the job of the flywheel?","options":["To seal the end of the crankshaft against oil leaks at the rear","To store energy, smooth the power pulses and carry the starter ring gear","To drive the camshaft at half the crankshaft speed using a chain","To pump oil to the main bearings under pressure through the block"],"answer":1,"level":2,"lesson":"m04-l4","explain":"The flywheel's inertia keeps the engine turning between power strokes."},
    {"id":"m04-q26","q":"Why is a bearing shell made slightly larger than its housing?","options":["So it is crushed tight into place by the cap","So it can slide around while the shaft turns","So it can wear down slowly over time before it fails","So it stays loose enough to allow oil to flow behind it"],"answer":0,"level":2,"lesson":"m04-l4","explain":"The crush holds the shell tight in the housing, prevents it from spinning and gives good heat transfer. The tab locates it."},
    {"id":"m04-q27","q":"What is blow-by?","options":["Air blown across the radiator by the cooling fan","Smoke from the exhaust when the engine is started","Gas leaking past the rings into the crankcase","Oil forced out of the engine by a blocked breather"],"answer":2,"level":1,"lesson":"m04-l5","explain":"Worn rings or bores let gas past the pistons, increasing crankcase pressure and oil mist at the breather, and reducing compression."},
    {"id":"m04-q28","q":"Blue smoke appears only when you start the engine from cold, and compression is good. What is the most likely cause?","options":["Worn piston rings that let oil past","A cracked cylinder head letting coolant in","A blocked fuel injector spraying too much fuel","Worn valve stem seals or guides"],"answer":3,"level":2,"lesson":"m04-l5","explain":"Oil leaks past worn valve seals while the engine is off and burns off on start-up. Rings would give smoke under load and poor compression."},
    {"id":"m04-q29","q":"What happens if a piston ring end gap is too small?","options":["The ends touch and the ring scuffs the bore","The ring falls out of its groove at high speed","The engine gets better compression than before","The ring needs a thicker oil to fill the gap"],"answer":0,"level":2,"lesson":"m04-l5","explain":"Rings expand with heat. If the gap closes completely, the ring bows against the wall and scores it. Measure the gap in the bore and file only as the manual allows."},
    {"id":"m04-q30","q":"Why should piston ring end gaps be spaced apart around the piston when it is fitted?","options":["So that the rings fit more easily into the cylinder","So that the engine can run in more quickly at the start","So gas and oil cannot leak straight through","So that the rings can rotate freely in their grooves"],"answer":2,"level":3,"lesson":"m04-l5","explain":"If the gaps lined up, combustion gas would take an easy path past all the rings. Staggering them forces gas along a longer route."},
    {"id":"m04-q31","q":"Where is the camshaft in a pushrod (OHV) engine?","options":["In the cylinder block, with pushrods and rockers moving the valves","On the outside of the block, turned by a separate small motor","In the cylinder head, directly above each valve with no pushrods","In the sump, below the crankshaft and driven by the oil pump"],"answer":0,"level":2,"lesson":"m04-l6","explain":"Overhead valve engines place the cam in the block and use pushrods to reach the valves in the head."},
    {"id":"m04-q32","q":"What happens if valve clearance is too small?","options":["The valve spring becomes weaker because of the extra gap","The valve opens too far and strikes the top of the piston","The valve may not close fully, so it burns and compression is lost","The engine becomes quieter, burns less fuel and runs cooler"],"answer":2,"level":2,"lesson":"m04-l6","explain":"Parts expand when hot. With too little clearance a hot valve can stay slightly open and burn on its seat."},
    {"id":"m04-q33","q":"What do hydraulic tappets do?","options":["They open the valves by using hydraulic fluid from the brake system","They adjust automatically using oil pressure, so they need no regular adjustment","They raise the valve springs when the engine is cold to ease starting","They set the ignition timing automatically at high engine speed"],"answer":1,"level":3,"lesson":"m04-l6","explain":"A small hydraulic piston takes up the clearance. Low oil pressure can make them noisy."},
    {"id":"m04-q34","q":"Why is valve clearance usually checked on a cold engine?","options":["A hot engine makes the feeler gauges expand and read too thick","The valves are held open by the cam while the engine is cold","The oil is too thin to measure the gap when it is hot","The specification is given for cold parts, before they expand"],"answer":3,"level":2,"lesson":"m04-l6","explain":"Specifications assume a stated temperature, normally cold. Always follow the manual."},
    {"id":"m04-q35","q":"What do valve stem seals do?","options":["They keep the exhaust gas out of the cooling system passages","They stop oil running down the valve stem into the cylinder","They hold the valve in its seat when the engine is cold and still","They hold the valve spring in place at the top of the stem"],"answer":1,"level":1,"lesson":"m04-l6","explain":"Worn stem seals let oil in, causing blue smoke at start-up."},
    {"id":"m04-q36","q":"Why should a cylinder head be loosened in the reverse of the tightening order?","options":["To make the bolts easier to count and put back later","To release the load evenly so the head does not warp","To allow the coolant to drain out of the head more quickly","To keep the bolts in the right order for the next job"],"answer":1,"level":1,"lesson":"m04-l7","explain":"Uneven release of stress can bend an aluminium head. Slacken the bolts a little at a time in a set pattern."},
    {"id":"m04-q37","q":"What is lapping a valve?","options":["Turning it on its seat with paste for a gas-tight seal","Cutting the valve stem to the correct length with a saw","Adding a thin layer of weld to the valve face","Heating the valve in oil to harden its seat"],"answer":0,"level":2,"lesson":"m04-l7","explain":"Fine paste polishes valve and seat into a perfect fit, which shows as a continuous grey band. Clean away all paste afterwards."},
    {"id":"m04-q38","q":"Why must blind bolt holes in the block be dry before the head bolts are fitted?","options":["Oil in the holes will make the bolts rust at the threads","Fluid in the holes would change the torque reading by half","Trapped fluid cannot compress and can crack the block","Wet threads reduce the effect of the head gasket sealant"],"answer":2,"level":3,"lesson":"m04-l7","explain":"Liquid cannot be squeezed into a smaller space, so a bolt driven into a hole full of it can generate enough pressure to crack the casting."},
    {"id":"m04-q39","q":"The old shim in a bucket tappet is 3.20 mm and the clearance is 0.12 mm too large. What shim thickness is needed?","options":["3.08 mm","3.32 mm","3.20 mm","3.44 mm"],"answer":1,"level":2,"lesson":"m04-l7","explain":"A thicker shim reduces clearance. Add the excess clearance to the old shim thickness: 3.20 + 0.12 = 3.32 mm."},
    {"id":"m04-q40","q":"What is an interference engine?","options":["An engine whose valves are noisy at idle and quiet on the move","An engine with extra radio noise from the ignition system leads","One where the valves and pistons can collide if the timing is lost","An engine that has two camshafts working against each other"],"answer":2,"level":2,"lesson":"m04-l8","explain":"If the belt breaks, the pistons can hit the open valves and bend them."},
    {"id":"m04-q41","q":"Why replace the water pump at the same time as the timing belt on many engines?","options":["The pump controls the timing marks on the camshaft sprocket","The new belt cannot be fitted with the old pump left in place","It is driven by the belt, and the labour to reach it is the same","The pump is made of rubber and wears out at the same rate"],"answer":2,"level":2,"lesson":"m04-l8","explain":"Most of the labour is shared. A seized pump could also break the new belt."},
    {"id":"m04-q42","q":"Before removing a timing belt, what should you do?","options":["Cut the belt off quickly and then mark the sprockets afterwards","Disconnect the spark plugs and leave the marks until later","Turn the crankshaft backwards several times to loosen the belt first","Check that the timing marks line up and photograph the routing"],"answer":3,"level":2,"lesson":"m04-l8","explain":"Marks and photographs let you refit the belt in the exact correct position."},
    {"id":"m04-q43","q":"After fitting a new timing belt, what must you do before starting the engine?","options":["Tighten the belt as much as possible with a long bar for safety","Start the engine straight away and listen carefully for the belt","Turn the engine by hand through two full turns and recheck the timing marks","Remove the spark plugs and run the starter motor for a full minute"],"answer":2,"level":3,"lesson":"m04-l8","explain":"Turning by hand checks that nothing is fouling and that the marks still line up."},
    {"id":"m04-q44","q":"A timing belt is cracked and has lost some teeth. How should it be rated on a job card?","options":["Not rated, because it is inside a cover","Red, because it can fail and may damage the engine","Amber, to be replaced at the next service in a year","Green, because it is still turning the camshaft"],"answer":1,"level":2,"lesson":"m04-l8","explain":"A damaged belt can fail at any time. On an interference engine, failure can wreck the valves."},
    {"id":"m04-q45","q":"A crankshaft sprocket has 19 teeth. How many teeth must the camshaft sprocket have?","options":["19","76","9.5","38"],"answer":3,"level":2,"lesson":"m04-l8","explain":"The camshaft turns at half crankshaft speed, so its sprocket has twice the teeth: 19 × 2 = 38."},
    {"id":"m04-q46","q":"After fitting a new timing belt, what should you do before starting the engine?","options":["Run it for two minutes to see whether it settles","Turn it by hand twice and check the marks","Spray the belt with oil to help it grip the sprockets","Tighten the tensioner as far as it will go"],"answer":1,"level":2,"lesson":"m04-l8","explain":"Turning it by hand shows whether the timing is correct and the valves clear the pistons. Starting an engine with the timing wrong can destroy it."},
    {"id":"m04-q47","q":"A rattle on a cold start that fades, together with a fault code about camshaft and crankshaft correlation, suggests what?","options":["A worn spark plug lead on the ignition system","A loose fuel cap letting air into the tank","Low windscreen washer fluid in the reservoir","A stretched chain, worn tensioner or phaser"],"answer":3,"level":3,"lesson":"m04-l8","explain":"Chain stretch and tensioner or phaser faults change the relative timing and make a cold-start rattle. The engine computer detects the difference between its two sensors."},
    {"id":"m04-q48","q":"What is the firing interval of a four-cylinder four-stroke engine?","options":["90° of crankshaft rotation","360° of crankshaft rotation","180° of crankshaft rotation","720° of crankshaft rotation"],"answer":2,"level":1,"lesson":"m04-l9","explain":"Each cylinder fires once per 720°, so with four cylinders a power stroke occurs every 720 ÷ 4 = 180°."},
    {"id":"m04-q49","q":"What does a dual-mass flywheel do?","options":["It absorbs torsional vibration before the gearbox","It stores fuel for cold starting the engine","It lets the gearbox change gear without the clutch","It spins faster than the crankshaft to add power"],"answer":0,"level":2,"lesson":"m04-l9","explain":"Two discs joined by springs absorb twist pulses. A worn one rattles at idle and shudders when the clutch is engaged."},
    {"id":"m04-q50","q":"A car shakes at idle, and the vibration eases when the engine speed is raised. What is a likely cause?","options":["A blocked fuel filter on the supply line","A discharged battery under the bonnet","Dirty windscreen wipers on the screen","A worn or collapsed engine mount"],"answer":3,"level":2,"lesson":"m04-l9","explain":"Worn mounts fail to absorb the idle vibration. At higher speed the frequency changes and the effect is less noticeable."},
    {"id":"m04-q51","q":"How should the cylinder head bolts be loosened?","options":["Gradually in the reverse of the tightening sequence","All together with an impact wrench at full power in any order","Starting with the bolt nearest the exhaust, then working to the inlet","Fully, one bolt at a time, starting at one end of the head"],"answer":0,"level":2,"lesson":"m04-l10","explain":"A gradual, reverse sequence prevents the head from warping."},
    {"id":"m04-q52","q":"A bore measures 80.04 mm at the top and 80.00 mm at the bottom. What is the taper?","options":["80.04 mm","0.4 mm","160.04 mm","0.04 mm"],"answer":3,"level":2,"lesson":"m04-l10","explain":"Taper is the top reading minus the bottom reading: 0.04 mm. Compare it with the limit."},
    {"id":"m04-q53","q":"How do you check a cylinder head for flatness?","options":["Lay a straightedge across it and try to slide a feeler gauge underneath","Weigh the head and compare it with the weight in the specification","Tap it with a hammer and listen to see whether it rings clearly","Measure its thickness with a steel rule at one point only"],"answer":0,"level":3,"lesson":"m04-l10","explain":"Gaps under a straightedge show warping. The manual gives the limit."},
    {"id":"m04-q54","q":"Why should small parts be bagged and labelled when dismantling?","options":["Because the parts become magnetic once they have been removed","So they can all be thrown away together at the end of the job","So the customer can count them one by one on the invoice later","So they go back in the correct place and nothing is lost"],"answer":3,"level":2,"lesson":"m04-l10","explain":"Matching parts, such as bearing shells and caps, must go back where they came from."},
    {"id":"m04-q55","q":"What problem can too much gasket sealant cause?","options":["It can squeeze into oil passages and block them","It will make the engine run hotter under every load","It will make the bolts tighten more easily to the torque","It will stop the gasket from ever leaking in future"],"answer":0,"level":2,"lesson":"m04-l10","explain":"Use sealant only where the manual says, and in the right amount."},
    {"id":"m04-q56","q":"Why must bearings never be washed in strong solvent?","options":["It can remove the surface layer and leave the bearing dry and damaged","Solvent is always too expensive to use on bearings in a workshop","Solvent makes the bearing expand too much to be fitted back in","Solvent leaves the bearing too clean to hold any oil afterwards"],"answer":0,"level":1,"lesson":"m04-l10","explain":"Clean bearings gently, handle them by the edges and oil them at assembly."},
    {"id":"m04-q57","q":"What should you do before starting a rebuilt engine for the first time?","options":["Fill the cylinders with fuel through the spark plug holes first","Disconnect the oil pressure switch so that no warning will show","Turn it by hand to check it moves freely and check the oil level","Run it at high speed for a few minutes to bed the parts in"],"answer":2,"level":2,"lesson":"m04-l10","explain":"A hand turn finds a locked or binding part before the starter can cause damage."},
    {"id":"m04-q58","q":"How does Plastigauge show bearing clearance?","options":["The colour of the strip shows the bearing size","Strip width is compared with a printed scale","The length of the strip equals the shaft diameter","The strip melts at a temperature linked to the clearance"],"answer":1,"level":2,"lesson":"m04-l10","explain":"The plastic thread flattens when the cap is tightened. The wider the strip, the smaller the clearance. The scale on the packet converts width to millimetres."},
    {"id":"m04-q59","q":"Why should you pre-fill and prime the oil system before the first start of a rebuilt engine?","options":["So the bearings are not dry at first start","To make the engine start more easily at first","To check the quality of the oil filter housing","To warm the pistons before they are pressed in"],"answer":0,"level":1,"lesson":"m04-l10","explain":"A dry bearing wears in seconds. Priming puts oil into the galleries and bearings before the first rotation."},
    {"id":"m04-q60","q":"Why must a stop-start vehicle have a battery of the specified type?","options":["It must survive many repeated starts","It makes the engine produce more power at the wheels","It lets the driver use a manual gearbox more easily","It reduces the weight of the vehicle by a large amount"],"answer":0,"level":2,"lesson":"m04-l11","explain":"An ordinary battery fails quickly under repeated deep cycling, and the control unit may disable the stop-start function. Use the type in the manual."},
    {"id":"m04-q61","q":"What problem can build up on the intake valves of a direct-injection petrol engine?","options":["Water, because the engine runs cooler than before","Rust, because the valves are made of plain steel","Carbon, since fuel no longer washes the valves","Fuel varnish from the cap on the filler tube"],"answer":2,"level":3,"lesson":"m04-l11","explain":"In a port-injection engine the fuel spray cleans the back of the valves. With direct injection that cleaning is gone, so oil vapour bakes into deposits that cause rough running."},
    {"id":"m04-q62","q":"What is cylinder deactivation?","options":["Switching off the whole engine whenever the vehicle stops moving","Shutting some cylinders at light load to cut pumping losses and save fuel","Adding extra cylinders to the engine when it must climb a steep hill","Disconnecting the spark plugs to reduce emissions while idling"],"answer":1,"level":2,"lesson":"m04-l11","explain":"At light load the engine uses a fraction of its power. Keeping some valves closed makes the remaining cylinders work harder at higher efficiency."},
    {"id":"m04-q63","q":"Why does a modern turbocharged downsized engine need correct oil and cooling maintenance?","options":["It uses far less oil than any larger engine would use","It has no oil filter fitted, so the oil is never cleaned","It runs at higher pressures and temperatures than a larger engine","It needs more coolant only because the radiator is smaller"],"answer":2,"level":3,"lesson":"m04-l11","explain":"Boost raises cylinder pressure and the turbocharger runs very hot. Poor oil or cooling shows up quickly as bearing and turbo damage."}
  ],
  "cards": [
    {"id":"m04-c01","front":"What does the crankshaft do?","back":"It converts the up-and-down motion of the pistons into rotation.","lesson":"m04-l1"},
    {"id":"m04-c02","front":"How does a diesel engine ignite its fuel?","back":"By compression. The air becomes so hot that the injected fuel ignites by itself.","lesson":"m04-l1"},
    {"id":"m04-c03","front":"How do you calculate swept volume?","back":"π ÷ 4 × bore² × stroke.","lesson":"m04-l1"},
    {"id":"m04-c04","front":"What is the firing order for?","back":"To balance the engine and space the power pulses evenly.","lesson":"m04-l1"},
    {"id":"m04-c05","front":"What advantage does a V layout have?","back":"It is shorter than an inline engine with the same number of cylinders.","lesson":"m04-l1"},
    {"id":"m04-c06","front":"What is TDC and BDC?","back":"Top dead centre and bottom dead centre, the highest and lowest positions of the piston.","lesson":"m04-l1"},
    {"id":"m04-c07","front":"What is an oversquare engine?","back":"One whose bore is larger than its stroke, which suits higher revolutions.","lesson":"m04-l1"},
    {"id":"m04-c08","front":"What are the four strokes in order?","back":"Intake, compression, power, exhaust.","lesson":"m04-l2"},
    {"id":"m04-c09","front":"How many crankshaft turns make one four-stroke cycle?","back":"Two turns, or 720°.","lesson":"m04-l2"},
    {"id":"m04-c10","front":"Why does the camshaft turn at half crankshaft speed?","back":"Each valve opens only once per two crankshaft turns.","lesson":"m04-l2"},
    {"id":"m04-c11","front":"What is valve overlap?","back":"A short time at the end of exhaust and start of intake when both valves are open.","lesson":"m04-l2"},
    {"id":"m04-c12","front":"Typical compression ratios of petrol and diesel engines?","back":"Petrol about 9:1 to 13:1. Diesel about 15:1 to 22:1.","lesson":"m04-l2"},
    {"id":"m04-c13","front":"What is torque and what is power?","back":"Torque is the turning force (N·m). Power is how fast work is done (kW) and equals torque times speed.","lesson":"m04-l3"},
    {"id":"m04-c14","front":"What is knock?","back":"Detonation: the last of the mixture self-ignites before the flame reaches it. It damages pistons and bearings.","lesson":"m04-l3"},
    {"id":"m04-c15","front":"What is octane and what is cetane?","back":"Octane measures resistance to knock in petrol. Cetane measures how easily diesel ignites.","lesson":"m04-l3"},
    {"id":"m04-c16","front":"Where does fuel energy go in a petrol engine?","back":"About a third each to work, exhaust heat and coolant, and a small share to friction.","lesson":"m04-l3"},
    {"id":"m04-c17","front":"What does the head gasket seal?","back":"The joint between the block and the head: combustion gas, oil and coolant.","lesson":"m04-l4"},
    {"id":"m04-c18","front":"What does the top piston ring do?","back":"It seals the combustion gas above the piston.","lesson":"m04-l4"},
    {"id":"m04-c19","front":"What does the oil control ring do?","back":"It scrapes surplus oil from the cylinder wall.","lesson":"m04-l4"},
    {"id":"m04-c20","front":"What carries the load on a plain bearing?","back":"A thin film of oil between the shell and the shaft.","lesson":"m04-l4"},
    {"id":"m04-c21","front":"What is the flywheel for?","back":"It stores energy, smooths the power pulses and carries the starter ring gear.","lesson":"m04-l4"},
    {"id":"m04-c22","front":"What is blow-by?","back":"Combustion gas leaking past the rings into the crankcase. It shows as oil mist and weak compression.","lesson":"m04-l5"},
    {"id":"m04-c23","front":"What causes blue smoke only at start-up?","back":"Worn valve stem seals or guides. Smoke under load suggests worn rings or bores.","lesson":"m04-l5"},
    {"id":"m04-c24","front":"Why must ring end gaps be staggered?","back":"So gas and oil cannot leak straight through all the rings.","lesson":"m04-l5"},
    {"id":"m04-c25","front":"What is valve clearance?","back":"A small gap that lets the valve close fully when the parts expand.","lesson":"m04-l6"},
    {"id":"m04-c26","front":"What happens if valve clearance is too small?","back":"The valve may not close, burn and lose compression.","lesson":"m04-l6"},
    {"id":"m04-c27","front":"What does a hydraulic tappet do?","back":"It uses oil pressure to take up the clearance automatically.","lesson":"m04-l6"},
    {"id":"m04-c28","front":"What is variable valve timing?","back":"A system that changes the camshaft position as the engine runs.","lesson":"m04-l6"},
    {"id":"m04-c29","front":"How should head bolts be tightened?","back":"In the specified sequence and in stages to the torque, plus angle if stated. Replace stretch bolts.","lesson":"m04-l7"},
    {"id":"m04-c30","front":"Why must blind bolt holes be dry?","back":"Trapped liquid cannot compress and can crack the block when the bolt is tightened.","lesson":"m04-l7"},
    {"id":"m04-c31","front":"How do shims set valve clearance?","back":"A thicker shim reduces clearance. A thinner one increases it. New shim = old shim + clearance error.","lesson":"m04-l7"},
    {"id":"m04-c32","front":"What is an interference engine?","back":"An engine in which valves and pistons collide if the timing is lost.","lesson":"m04-l8"},
    {"id":"m04-c33","front":"Why replace the water pump with the timing belt?","back":"It is often driven by the belt and the labour is shared.","lesson":"m04-l8"},
    {"id":"m04-c34","front":"What should you check after fitting a timing belt?","back":"Turn the engine by hand through two full turns and check the marks.","lesson":"m04-l8"},
    {"id":"m04-c35","front":"What does a timing chain failure sound like?","back":"A rattle on a cold start. Codes may report camshaft and crankshaft correlation faults.","lesson":"m04-l8"},
    {"id":"m04-c36","front":"What is the firing interval of an inline six?","back":"120° of crankshaft rotation. Typical firing order is 1-5-3-6-2-4.","lesson":"m04-l9"},
    {"id":"m04-c37","front":"Signs of a bad engine mount?","back":"Idle vibration, clunks on accelerating or lifting off, and the engine moving a lot when the throttle is blipped.","lesson":"m04-l9"},
    {"id":"m04-c38","front":"How should head bolts be loosened?","back":"Gradually, in the reverse of the tightening sequence.","lesson":"m04-l10"},
    {"id":"m04-c39","front":"What is bore taper?","back":"The difference between the readings at the top and the bottom of the bore.","lesson":"m04-l10"},
    {"id":"m04-c40","front":"How do you check a head for flatness?","back":"With a straightedge and a feeler gauge.","lesson":"m04-l10"},
    {"id":"m04-c41","front":"Why must gasket sealant be used sparingly?","back":"It can squeeze into oil passages and block them.","lesson":"m04-l10"},
    {"id":"m04-c42","front":"What is the most important habit in engine assembly?","back":"Cleanliness. One piece of grit can ruin a bearing.","lesson":"m04-l10"},
    {"id":"m04-c43","front":"Why do direct-injection engines get carbon on intake valves?","back":"Fuel is sprayed into the cylinder, so it no longer washes the back of the valves.","lesson":"m04-l11"},
    {"id":"m04-c44","front":"What is the Atkinson or Miller cycle?","back":"The intake valve closes late so expansion is longer than compression. It is efficient but less powerful, and is common in hybrids.","lesson":"m04-l11"}
  ]
});
